{"name":"Corn Maze","hex":"#deaa6e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"corn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any cereal plant (or its grain) that is the main crop or staple of a country or region.","Maize, a grain crop of the species Zea mays."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To granulate; to form (a substance) into grains.","To preserve using coarse salt, e.g. corned beef."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/corn"},{"term":"maze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A labyrinth; a puzzle consisting of a complicated network of paths or passages, the aim of which is to find one's way through.","Something made up of many confused or conflicting elements; a tangle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To amaze, astonish, bewilder.","To daze or stupefy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/maze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Corn maze","blocks":[{"text":"A corn maze or maize maze is a maze cut out of a corn field. Corn mazes have become popular agritourism attractions in North America, and are a way for farms to generate tourist income. Corn mazes appear in many different designs. Most have a path which goes all around the whole pattern, either to end in the middle or to come back out again, with various false trails diverging from the main path. In the United Kingdom, they are known as maize mazes, and are especially popular with farms in the east of England."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Maislabyrinth_%2805%29_%2819665624374%29.jpg/330px-Maislabyrinth_%2805%29_%2819665624374%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corn_maze"},{"title":"Maize","blocks":[{"text":"Maize, also known as corn, is a tall stout grass that produces cereal grain. The leafy stalk of the plant gives rise to male inflorescences or tassels which produce pollen, and female inflorescences called ears. The ears yield grain, known as kernels or seeds. In modern commercial varieties, these are usually yellow or white; other varieties can be of many colors. Maize was domesticated by indigenous peoples in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago from wild teosinte. Native Americans planted it alongside beans and squashes in the Three Sisters polyculture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize"},{"title":"Maze","blocks":[{"text":"A maze is a path or collection of paths, typically from an entrance to a goal. The word is used to refer both to branching tour puzzles through which the solver must find a route, and to simpler non-branching (\"unicursal\") patterns that lead unambiguously through a convoluted layout to a goal. The term \"labyrinth\" is generally synonymous with \"maze\", but can also connote specifically a unicursal pattern. The pathways and walls in a maze are typically fixed, but puzzles in which the walls and paths can change during the game are also categorised as mazes or tour puzzles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Longleat-maze.jpg/330px-Longleat-maze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maze"},{"title":"Maize (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The shade maize or corn refers to a specific tone of yellow; it is named for the cereal of the same name—maize. In public usage, maize can be applied to a variety of shades, ranging from light yellow to a dark shade that borders on orange, since the color of maize may vary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Cornheap.jpg/330px-Cornheap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Inside_a_corn_maze_near_Christchurch%2C_New_Zealand.JPG/500px-Inside_a_corn_maze_near_Christchurch%2C_New_Zealand.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Inside_a_corn_maze_near_Christchurch%2C_New_Zealand.JPG/1280px-Inside_a_corn_maze_near_Christchurch%2C_New_Zealand.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Inside_a_corn_maze_near_Christchurch%2C_New_Zealand.JPG/500px-Inside_a_corn_maze_near_Christchurch%2C_New_Zealand.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A view from inside a corn maze near Christchurch, New Zealand","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInside_a_corn_maze_near_Christchurch%2C_New_Zealand.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Grand_labyrinthe_Kamouraska%2C_La_Pocati%C3%A8re%2C_Canada.jpg/500px-Grand_labyrinthe_Kamouraska%2C_La_Pocati%C3%A8re%2C_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Grand_labyrinthe_Kamouraska%2C_La_Pocati%C3%A8re%2C_Canada.jpg/1280px-Grand_labyrinthe_Kamouraska%2C_La_Pocati%C3%A8re%2C_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Grand_labyrinthe_Kamouraska%2C_La_Pocati%C3%A8re%2C_Canada.jpg/500px-Grand_labyrinthe_Kamouraska%2C_La_Pocati%C3%A8re%2C_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The largest corn maze in the world,[1] according to Guinness World Records. Mazes can be designed artistically.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrand_labyrinthe_Kamouraska%2C_La_Pocati%C3%A8re%2C_Canada.jpg"